The total momentum of the system is
$
  bold(P) = sum_(i=1)^N bold(p_i) = M bold(dot(R)),
$
so the centre of mass moves as if it is a single particle of mass $M$. Since internal forces cancel pairwise,
$
  M bold(dot.double(R)) = bold(F) = sum_i bold(F_i^"ext"),
$
so the centre of mass accelerates like a point particle subject to the external force $bold(F)$. In particular, $bold(F) = bold(0)$ implies $bold(dot(P)) = bold(0)$: the total momentum of a system is conserved in the absence of external forces. This is why an extended object such as the Earth can be treated as a point particle: its internal forces cancel out.
